  "articleBody": "Creating a truly slip-resistant shower floor isn't just about picking a 'non-slip' tile; it's about a combination of factors that work together to keep you safe. When you're planning a shower remodel, keeping these points in mind will make a big difference.\n\nFirst, focus on a **textured surface**. Smooth, glossy tiles might look nice on a showroom wall, but in a wet, soapy shower, they can become incredibly slick. Textured porcelain or natural stone with a honed finish are often good choices. Second, think about **dense grout lines**. Small-format tiles, like mosaics, penny tiles, or pebble styles, naturally create more grout lines. These lines act as tiny channels, helping water drain and providing extra grip underfoot. This is why smaller tiles are often preferred for shower pans.\n\nFinally, and perhaps most crucially, ensure proper **drainage slope**. Even the most slip-resistant surface can become hazardous if water pools on it. Your shower floor needs to be correctly sloped towards the drain so that water runs off quickly, minimizing standing water. We recommend aiming for a wet DCOF of about 0.50-0.60+ for shower floors to ensure ample safety. If you're planning a shower remodel in Denver or a surrounding area and want to get it right, HH Remodeling LLC can help you select the best materials and ensure expert installation for maximum safety.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
